Stop provider mode notifications from flooding the session with metadata writes.

Each current_mode_update or session_modes_state notification from an ACP agent queued a
full session metadata write stamped with Date.now(), even when the mode had not changed.
During the session mode override retry loop, Copilot answered every rejected mode change
with such a notification, and stored them in its session history. Resuming that session
replayed them: 438,153 queued writes of about 37.5 KB each, draining at 18 per second, which
the relay then fanned out to every connected client for hours.

This patch ports the fork's fix to the bundled CLI 0.2.20 package:
- The two mode handlers return the metadata unchanged when the provider repeats the
  published modes.
- A runtime publication that would not change the current metadata is not queued.
- While one runtime publication waits in the session write queue, later ones join it and
  are applied in order when it runs, so a burst costs one write. A reset starts a fresh
  batch, and a write that fails before running releases it.
- updateMetadataBestEffort returns its promise so the runtime can release the batch.

Apply from the extracted release root with patch -p1 --fuzz=0, after
copilot-per-model-reasoning-effort.patch and session-mode-override-retry-backoff.patch.
The content-addressed chunk names and hunks are specific to cli-v0.2.20.

Adapted from the fork's apps/cli/src/agent/acp/runtime/createAcpRuntime.ts and
apps/cli/src/api/session/sessionWritesBestEffort.ts.

